<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · island</p><h1 class="enc-title">Islay</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Islay is the southernmost island of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inner-hebrides">Inner Hebrides</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a>. In the early <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a> it was the home of the Cenél nÓengusa of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dal-riata">Dál Riata</a>, and from the 14th century it was the heart of the Lordship of the Isles, ruled from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/finlaggan">Finlaggan</a>. It is now known for its farming, its birdlife and its whisky distilleries.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/islay.jpg" alt="Islay" width="320" height="238" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: J M Briscoe · CC BY-SA 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Port_Ellen,_Islay.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Islay lies in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/argyll">Argyll</a> and Bute just south-west of Jura and about 40 kilometres north of the Northern Irish coast. It is the fifth-largest Scottish island, with an area of almost 620 square kilometres, 40 kilometres long from north to south and 24 kilometres broad. The east coast is rugged and mountainous, rising steeply from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oresund">the Sound</a> of Islay, while the fertile south-western arm is called The Rinns and a rocky peninsula, The Oa, is the closest point in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebrides">Hebrides</a> to Ireland. The Gulf Stream keeps the climate mild, though winter gales off the Atlantic can gust up to 185 kilometres per hour. Its capital is Bowmore and its main port is Port Ellen.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The earliest settlers were hunter-gatherers who arrived after the retreat of the ice caps. A flint arrowhead found near Bridgend in 1993, dated to 10,800 BCE, is among the earliest evidence of human presence yet found in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesolithic">Mesolithic</a> finds date to 7000 BCE. By the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neolithic">Neolithic</a> settlement was more permanent and communal monuments were built. Ptolemy probably recorded the island as Epidion, and in the 7th century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adomnan">Adomnán</a> called it Ilea. The root of the name is not Gaelic and is of unknown origin.</p><p>By the sixth century CE Islay lay within the Gaelic kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dal-riata">Dál Riata</a>, which had strong links to Ireland. According to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/senchus-fer-n-alban">Senchus fer n-Alban</a>, the Cenél nÓengusa controlled Islay and Jura. The arrival of Scandinavian settlers in the ninth century began with the destruction of Dál Riata, and place names suggest a complete break from the past, although Gaelic survived as a spoken language of low status. The Norse set up the Kingdom of the Isles, which became part of the crown of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norway">Norway</a>, and its rulers were mostly of Norse origin for four centuries and more.</p><p>The transfer of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebrides">Hebrides</a> to the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a> was followed by the emergence of the Clan Donald Lordship of the Isles, originally centred at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/finlaggan">Finlaggan</a>, a period described as a cultural high point. At the end of the 13th century the MacDougalls backed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-balliol">John Balliol</a> against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/robert-the-bruce">Robert the Bruce</a>, and when Robert won, he declared their lands forfeit and gave them to the MacDonalds and the Macruari. In the 17th century the power of Clan Donald waned. Improvements to agriculture and transport raised the population to a peak in the mid-19th century, followed by substantial forced displacements and falling numbers.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Islay has over 3,000 inhabitants, and about a quarter of them speak Scottish Gaelic. Its main activities are agriculture, malt whisky distilling, fishing and tourism. Wintering populations of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greenland">Greenland</a> white-fronted and barnacle geese make it a destination for birdwatchers.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Islay” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Senchus fer nAlban names three main kindreds in Britain: Cenél nGabráin, centred in Kintyre; Cenél Loairn, in Lorn to the north; and Cenél nÓengusa on <b>Islay</b>, while Cenél Comgaill in Cowal appears in the annals as a further group.</blockquote><a href="/empire/dal-riata">Dal Riata · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>The lords held council at Finlaggan on <b>Islay</b>, with a council of the isles, and patronised the Iona abbey, galley-building, Gaelic poets and physicians.</blockquote><a href="/state/lordship-of-the-isles">Lordship of the Isles</a></li></ul></section>
